{"id": "language-p00122", "blocks": [{"id": "language-p00122", "text": "5. Trit-to-Melody Algorithm", "style": "HEADING_1", "sourceParagraph": 122}, {"id": "language-p00123", "text": "Trits are encoded as relative melodic movement, not as fixed notes and not as the root, third, and fifth of a chord.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 123}, {"id": "language-p00124", "text": "Trit 0: remain on the current pitch; movement 0 semitones", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 124}, {"id": "language-p00125", "text": "Trit 1: rise one DBA step; movement +2 semitones", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 125}, {"id": "language-p00126", "text": "Trit 2: fall one DBA step; movement -2 semitones", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 126}, {"id": "language-p00127", "text": "One DBA step: 2 semitones", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 127}, {"id": "language-p00128", "text": "A note path always contains the starting pitch followed by one movement for each trit. A three-trit root therefore has four pitch positions.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 128}, {"id": "language-p00129", "text": "000: relative path [0, 0, 0, 0]", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 129}, {"id": "language-p00130", "text": "012: relative path [0, 0, +2, 0]", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 130}, {"id": "language-p00131", "text": "111: relative path [0, +2, +4, +6]", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 131}, {"id": "language-p00132", "text": "222: relative path [0, -2, -4, -6]", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 132}, {"id": "language-p00133", "text": "Example: 012 from C is C–C–D–C. The same address from G is G–G–A–G. Both are the same DBA root because the interval sequence is identical.", "style": "NORMAL_TEXT", "sourceParagraph": 133}], "outline": [{"id": "language-p00122", "title": "5. Trit-to-Melody Algorithm", "level": 1}]}